WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.431
[HAND CLAPPING]

00:00:01.431 --> 00:00:03.339
[GUITAR STRUMMING]

00:00:04.770 --> 00:00:06.690
[CHIRPING]

00:00:07.782 --> 00:00:09.240
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
Want to know how

00:00:09.240 --> 00:00:11.220
I built this without any code?

00:00:11.220 --> 00:00:13.108
Stay tuned.

00:00:13.108 --> 00:00:15.084
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:21.655 --> 00:00:23.320
Welcome to "AI
Adventures," where

00:00:23.320 --> 00:00:26.620
we explore the art, science,
and tools of machine learning.

00:00:26.620 --> 00:00:30.700
And today, I'm going to show you
how to train a sound classifier

00:00:30.700 --> 00:00:33.340
with absolutely no code.

00:00:33.340 --> 00:00:35.980
We are going to use
Teachable Machines for this.

00:00:35.980 --> 00:00:38.950
Teachable Machines allows us
to train a machine learning

00:00:38.950 --> 00:00:41.140
model right in the browser.

00:00:41.140 --> 00:00:43.370
We can train it
on images, audio,

00:00:43.370 --> 00:00:45.670
and poses, with more to come.

00:00:45.670 --> 00:00:47.920
We gather the data,
train the model,

00:00:47.920 --> 00:00:51.010
and export it to use
it in our own projects.

00:00:51.010 --> 00:00:53.230
That's exactly what
we will do today.

00:00:53.230 --> 00:00:56.560
We will create an audio
project, record some samples

00:00:56.560 --> 00:00:57.880
of background noise.

00:00:57.880 --> 00:00:59.830
You might see a
browser notification

00:00:59.830 --> 00:01:02.920
to allow using the
microphone, and I have already

00:01:02.920 --> 00:01:04.069
done that here.

00:01:04.069 --> 00:01:06.400
So once the audio
is recorded, we

00:01:06.400 --> 00:01:10.300
extract these samples,
which are one second each.

00:01:10.300 --> 00:01:13.840
For background noise,
it requires 20 samples.

00:01:13.840 --> 00:01:15.750
Then take a few samples of--

00:01:15.750 --> 00:01:16.420
[HAND CLAPPING]

00:01:16.420 --> 00:01:19.510
Clapping sound, and
we label them as clap.

00:01:19.510 --> 00:01:23.320
And here, it needs eight
samples of one second each.

00:01:23.320 --> 00:01:26.200
Let's record a few
samples for guitar sound.

00:01:26.200 --> 00:01:28.020
[GUITAR STRUMMING]

00:01:30.750 --> 00:01:34.390
Now for our last label,
let's record a few bird

00:01:34.390 --> 00:01:35.340
sound samples.

00:01:35.340 --> 00:01:38.740
I have this bird that I
got from trip to Mexico,

00:01:38.740 --> 00:01:41.503
and it makes this
beautiful bird chirp sound.

00:01:41.503 --> 00:01:43.355
[CHIRPING]

00:01:45.210 --> 00:01:47.970
Once our labels are
recorded, all we need to do

00:01:47.970 --> 00:01:49.530
is to train the model.

00:01:49.530 --> 00:01:51.880
Since this is
browser-based training,

00:01:51.880 --> 00:01:53.760
make sure to not
close the window.

00:01:53.760 --> 00:01:55.860
While the model is
training, you might

00:01:55.860 --> 00:01:58.720
be wondering what's
happening under the hood.

00:01:58.720 --> 00:02:00.400
Well, there is a lot happening.

00:02:00.400 --> 00:02:02.700
But to give you a quick
sense, these models

00:02:02.700 --> 00:02:05.410
use a technique called
transfer learning.

00:02:05.410 --> 00:02:07.610
There's a pre-trained
neural network,

00:02:07.610 --> 00:02:10.710
and when you create your
own classes or labels,

00:02:10.710 --> 00:02:14.160
the properties of these
classes become the last layer

00:02:14.160 --> 00:02:15.630
of that neural network.

00:02:15.630 --> 00:02:19.230
Specially, both the
image and pause models

00:02:19.230 --> 00:02:22.230
learn off of the pre-trained
MobileNet models,

00:02:22.230 --> 00:02:26.730
and the sound model is built
on speech command recognizer.

00:02:26.730 --> 00:02:28.450
If you are curious
to dive deeper,

00:02:28.450 --> 00:02:31.710
there's a blog linked
below just for you.

00:02:31.710 --> 00:02:33.780
And it is trained.

00:02:33.780 --> 00:02:35.880
We can test it out right here.

00:02:35.880 --> 00:02:37.590
Let me make the bird sound.

00:02:37.590 --> 00:02:39.640
[CHIRPING]

00:02:40.140 --> 00:02:44.280
And it rightly identifies
it as our bird label.

00:02:44.280 --> 00:02:45.150
If I clap--

00:02:45.150 --> 00:02:46.440
[HAND CLAPPING]

00:02:46.440 --> 00:02:48.510
It identifies it as clap.

00:02:48.510 --> 00:02:49.740
And if I play guitar--

00:02:49.740 --> 00:02:51.528
[GUITAR STRUMMING]

00:02:52.960 --> 00:02:55.000
It identifies that too.

00:02:55.000 --> 00:02:57.010
Cool, right?

00:02:57.010 --> 00:02:58.690
Now that we are
happy with our model,

00:02:58.690 --> 00:03:01.240
we can upload it to
be hosted or download

00:03:01.240 --> 00:03:03.550
it to use directly in our app.

00:03:03.550 --> 00:03:05.950
On uploading, we get
a link to our model

00:03:05.950 --> 00:03:08.470
that we can copy
and use in our apps.

00:03:08.470 --> 00:03:11.500
Let's test it right
here in p5.js.

00:03:11.500 --> 00:03:14.540
Replace our model
URL, and if we run it,

00:03:14.540 --> 00:03:16.630
it shows the label text.

00:03:16.630 --> 00:03:19.830
Now, to make the output a
little bit more interesting,

00:03:19.830 --> 00:03:23.380
let's add some fun
emojis for each label.

00:03:23.380 --> 00:03:25.540
And let's test it out again.

00:03:25.540 --> 00:03:31.300
We play some guitar, we play
the bird chirp, and we clap.

00:03:31.300 --> 00:03:33.400
Now this is cool,
but I wanted to use

00:03:33.400 --> 00:03:35.450
this as a web application.

00:03:35.450 --> 00:03:38.590
So I downloaded this
HTML and JavaScript

00:03:38.590 --> 00:03:41.470
from p5.js web
editor and decided

00:03:41.470 --> 00:03:44.920
to host a simple node.js
web app on Cloud Run.

00:03:44.920 --> 00:03:47.410
Pointed the index.js
file to call

00:03:47.410 --> 00:03:51.430
the index.js HTML that
was downloaded from p5.js,

00:03:51.430 --> 00:03:53.120
and that's it.

00:03:53.120 --> 00:03:56.440
And here is the web app that
can now predict the sounds

00:03:56.440 --> 00:03:58.060
that we trained it on.

00:03:58.060 --> 00:03:59.680
If you are interested
in the code,

00:03:59.680 --> 00:04:02.260
I have linked the
GitHub repository below.

00:04:02.260 --> 00:04:04.460
And that's how you do it.

00:04:04.460 --> 00:04:06.940
We just trained a sound
classification model

00:04:06.940 --> 00:04:10.030
with literally no code,
applied minor changes

00:04:10.030 --> 00:04:13.420
to make our predictions fun,
and then finally deployed it

00:04:13.420 --> 00:04:14.710
on Cloud Run.

00:04:14.710 --> 00:04:16.540
If you're feeling
adventurous and would

00:04:16.540 --> 00:04:18.880
like to get deeper into
the sound classification

00:04:18.880 --> 00:04:22.505
techniques, I've included a
link to the tutorial below.

00:04:22.505 --> 00:04:24.130
Thanks for watching,
and I look forward

00:04:24.130 --> 00:04:28.000
to seeing you next time with
another tool in the AI toolkit.

00:04:28.000 --> 00:04:29.590
Until then.

00:04:29.590 --> 00:04:32.040
[CHIRPING]

